    {
      "claim_id": "PMC13054752-mech-001",
      "claim_text": "The paper reports phenotype-level bacteriocin activity against MRSA by agar spot testing: E. asini and E. saccharolyticus bacteriocins inhibited MRSA growth at 100-200 AU/mL; L. lactis bacteriocins L.l MK 2/8, L.l MK 2/2, and L.l MK 2/7 inhibited at 100-200 AU/mL; and nisin had the highest reported inhibitory activity at 100-800 AU/mL.",
      "direct_assay_types": [],
      "entity_scope": {
        "assay_scope": "agar spot complete growth-inhibition titer expressed in AU/mL",
        "entities": [
          "Ent. as. 1 from Enterococcus asini EAs 1/11D27",
          "Ent. sacch. from Enterococcus saccharolyticus Es 3/11D27",
          "L.l MK 2/8 from Lactococcus lactis",
          "L.l MK 2/2 from Lactococcus lactis",
          "L.l MK 2/7 from Lactococcus lactis",
          "nisin from Lactococcus lactis (Nisaplin)"
        ],
        "entity_type": "bacteriocins or bacteriocin-like inhibitory substances",
        "mechanism_scope": "growth inhibition phenotype, not a target-closed or membrane-specific mechanism",
        "target": "methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus strains",
        "target_scope": "MRSA strains, n=40; 39 pig isolates and 1 cow isolate"
      },
      "evidence_class": "phenotype_supported",
      "limitation_notes": [
        "Agar spot growth inhibition is an activity phenotype and is not direct mechanism evidence.",
        "The paper reports AU/mL ranges, not MIC/MBC, membrane permeability, membrane potential, binding, structural, microscopy, or target-enzyme assays for the bacteriocins.",
